all right i want to take some time and  talk about gas prices gas prices are  high there's no denying it but the level  of hyperbole and drama and the sky is  falling crazy statements from right-wing  media are starting to really get to be a  little bit too much let me give you just  an absurd example of this fox news has  an anchor named sandra smith  she says that gas prices were keeping  her up at night guys listen i understand  being concerned about inflation i  understand being concerned about the  cost of shipping things that go by truck  because of gas prices this is a real  issue and i've been taking it seriously  we're supposed to believe sandra smith  was up at night she couldn't sleep  because of gas prices listen to this as  you said pollen kept you up last night  you know it kept me up last night can we  even can we even  tap into the spr  in the quantity the capacity  that we're talking about is it possible  a million barrels a day so i reached out  to pipeline workers i reached out to  those in the industry as many as i could  we have never historically tapped that  amount is it possible the answer i'm  being given is no okay so first of all i  don't believe this was keeping her up at  night and if it were it'd be a little  bit weird number two  now the concern is oh we're now we are  tapping the strategic petroleum reserve  that's not good but before the issue was  why isn't joe biden tapping into the sbr  so i'm gonna break this down for you  piece by piece first a really quick word  from our sponsor morning brew if you're  like me and i know uh many of you are in  different ways you probably spend some  of your day kind of mindlessly scrolling  through twitter  or reddit and kind of coming across news  articles but not really taking in  what's going on in the world today  morning brew lets you soak up the entire  day's news  in a focused way that takes about five  minutes a day it's a way better use of  your time morning brew is a free daily  newsletter it's seven days a week in  about five minutes morning brew gets me  caught up on politics business finance  tech just what's going on in the world  in a much more focused and efficient way  much better use of my time they take all  the day's news stories and boil them  down into  just the most important points so you'll  be informed about what's taking place  maybe you like watching the david pakman  show because i take stories  hopefully break them down and give you  the important takeaways and something to  think about that's exactly what morning  brew does but morning brew will keep you  in the loop on more than just politics  like i said when you have a few minutes  of downtime on your phone  don't get bogged down in either clicking  around or one long news article you're  never actually going to finish that's  where morning brew is perfect you get a  well-crafted  series of bite-size articles and you get  through them quickly and you're informed  subscribe to morningbrew right now using  the link below alright so let's take  this seriously gas prices are high  it's not joe biden's fault as i've said  before and when gas prices were higher  lower under obama bush trump presidents  have very little impact over gas prices  as i've said presidents can basically do  three things one they can go to war with  a country where we get oil that'll make  gas prices go up they can argue for a  gas tax break which will make gas prices  go down  and they can release some oil from the  strategic petroleum reserve which will  impact gas prices somewhere in the range  of 5 to 15 cents per gallon now the  first question is  how high are gas prices really the first  step is many people will say well let's  just look at the nominal price of gas if  it's four dollars a gallon now  and it's never been higher than 399 in  the past then gas prices are higher now  but that doesn't take into account  inflation but the problem is  inflation-adjusted gas prices alone  aren't actually that great of a measure  at determining how gas prices are in  reality today so another measure that  can be looked at is  if we account for wages how many minutes  of work are required to purchase a  gallon of gas that's an interesting  metric and if you look at that gas  prices right now  are nowhere near as high as they were in  2008 in 2011 in 2012 2013 2014 they are  getting there they may get there and  i'll i will tell you if that happens but  if we really want to take a more  systemic look at what sort of cost gas  prices represent  there are many times in the past where  the average worker needed to work longer  to buy one gallon of gas  another metric that's interesting is  what is the real price of gas per mile  driven why would we look at that well  even as gas prices go up and down  vehicles have become more efficient over  time and so you might have a higher  nominal gas price  it may be higher or similar in inflation  adjusted terms it may relate to how much  work do you need to do in that you need  to work 10 minutes to afford a gallon of  gas versus 8 but also because cars on  average are more expensive what is the  real cost per mile driven because if you  have a 10 mile commute  and gas it costs more but you need less  of it to make the 10 hour drive  that's also an important metric and if  you look at that as well because  vehicles are significantly more  efficient now  the real price of gas per mile driven  is dramatically lower than it was in the  80s and it is also lower than it was  around 2008 2011 2012 2013.  does this mean we shouldn't care about  high gas prices of course we should of  course we should and i have never hidden  from you what the price of gas is i've  also been consistent that presidents  have very little impact on the price of  gas this gets us to the strategic  petroleum reserve uh joe biden will  start releasing one million barrels of  oil per day  from the reserves  to help cut gas prices this is something  many republicans were demanding now  they're worried we don't have the oil  for it they are bad faith actors but the  most important thing about this is like  i've told you many times before  the estimate  is as a result of this tapping of the  strategic petroleum reserve  gas prices would drop between 5 and 15  cents per gallon this is a global issue  we knew  that's all that would happen  based on opening up the strategic  petroleum reserve and that is exactly  what economists are now telling us

